.\" Process this file with
.\" groff -man -Tascii rename.2
.\"
.TH RENAME 2 "2026-02-28" "Substrate Kernel" "System Calls Manual"
.SH NAME
rename \- change the name or location of a file
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library (libc, -lc)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int rename(const char *" oldpath ", const char *" newpath ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rename
() renames a file, moving it between directories if required.
Any other hard links to the file (as created using
.BR link (2))
are unaffected. Open file descriptors for
.I oldpath
are also unaffected.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned. On error, -1 is returned.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EACCES
Write or search permission is denied for the directory containing
.I oldpath
or
.IR newpath ,
or
.I oldpath
is a directory being moved to a new parent and the caller lacks write
permission on it (its
.B ..
entry must change).
.TP
.B EROFS
The file is on a read-only mount.
.TP
.B EPERM
The directory containing
.I oldpath
(or containing an existing
.IR newpath )
has the sticky bit set, and the caller owns neither that directory nor
the entry (and is not the superuser).
.TP
.B EEXIST
.I newpath
already exists and is not empty.
.TP
.B ENOENT
.I oldpath
does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
.B Program source
.nf

#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    // Rename a file from "old.txt" to "new.txt"
    if (rename("old.txt", "new.txt") == 0) {
        printf("File renamed successfully.\\n");
    } else {
        perror("rename");
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
.fi

